# Radiant Cut Solitaire Engagement Rings: The 4 vs 6 Prong Rule

Radiant cut solitaire engagement rings put every bit of attention on the stone. No halo frame. No pavé sparkle on the shank. Just the radiant cut, held by a small number of prongs, doing everything on its own.

That's exactly why buyers choose them — and exactly why the decisions matter more. A solitaire exposes your color grade more than any other setting. It also forces a prong count choice that most buyers never know they need to make until they're looking at the ring on their finger.

This post covers both decisions with real certified market pricing, a full setting comparison across all metals and styles, and a color grade guide built specifically for radiant solitaires. Every price is stone + setting combined. Every table has a "What this means" column.

**TL;DR:**
- Radiant cut solitaire engagement rings start at [$1,867 lab](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29417414?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) or [$3,318 natural](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/25995245?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) (stone + setting)
- → Best entry: [1ct E-VVS1 IGI lab, $867](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29417414?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) + [Petite Solitaire 14K WG, $1,000](https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/petite-solitaire-engagement-ring-in-14k-white-gold-item-194386?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) = **$1,867**
- 4-prong: maximum face-up stone visible, corner-set, modern look
- 6-prong: more corner coverage, classic look, extra security
- Color floor: G for white gold solitaire; H works fine in yellow or rose gold
- No halo comparison pressure — G is fully sufficient, buying F is preference not necessity

